::STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A dataset records only the number of books each student read last month. What type of data structure is this?
{
=A. Univariate, because one variable is recorded for each student#The one variable is number of books read.
~B. Bivariate, because many students are included#Many cases do not mean there are two variables.
~C. Categorical bivariate, because books have categories#The dataset records a count, not book categories.
~D. Neither, because books are not numerical#The number of books is a numerical count.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A study records each student's hours of sleep and reaction time. What display is most appropriate for exploring the relationship?
{
=A. Scatterplot#A scatterplot shows the relationship between two numerical variables.
~B. Bar chart#A bar chart compares categories, but both variables are numerical.
~C. Histogram#A histogram displays one numerical variable, not a relationship.
~D. Two-way table#Two-way tables are usually for two categorical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A table records grade level and preferred lunch option for each student. What kind of data relationship can be studied?
{
~A. A relationship between two numerical variables#Neither variable is a measured numerical quantity.
~B. A distribution of one numerical variable#There are two variables, and both are categorical.
=C. A relationship between two categorical variables#Grade level and lunch option are both categories.
~D. No relationship, because categories cannot be compared#Categorical variables can be compared using counts or relative frequencies.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display best fits one numerical variable such as commute time in minutes?
{
~A. Two-way table#A two-way table is usually for two categorical variables.
~B. Segmented bar graph#A segmented bar graph compares categorical relative frequencies.
~C. Scatterplot#A scatterplot requires two numerical variables.
=D. Dot plot#A dot plot can show the distribution of one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Why is favorite sport and number of hours practiced per week a bivariate dataset?
{
~A. Because the sport names are longer than the numbers#Text length is not what makes data bivariate.
=B. Because two variables are recorded for each person#Each person has a sport category and a numerical practice-time value.
~C. Because every dataset with students is bivariate#A dataset can include many students and still have only one variable.
~D. Because all sports data are numerical#Favorite sport is categorical, while practice time is numerical.
}
